# Callbacks and 'Callback Hell' 🌋

Before Promises, asynchronous code relied entirely on nested callbacks:

```javascript
// The infamous "Pyramid of Doom"
getUser(userId, (user) => {
  getOrders(user.id, (orders) => {
    getOrderDetails(orders[0].id, (details) => {
      calculateTotal(details, (total) => {
        console.log("Total:", total);
      });
    });
  });
});
```

Deep nesting made error handling, readability, and debugging nightmarish!

# Introduction to Promises 🤝

A **Promise** is an object representing the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation.

## The 3 Promise States:
1. **Pending:** Initial state, neither fulfilled nor rejected.
2. **Fulfilled:** Operation completed successfully (`resolve(data)`).
3. **Rejected:** Operation failed (`reject(error)`).

```javascript
const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  const success = true;
  if (success) {
    resolve("Data retrieved successfully!");
  } else {
    reject(new Error("Network failure"));
  }
});

myPromise.then(data => console.log(data));
```

