# Modern `async` and `await` ⚡

Introduced in ES2017, `async` / `await` allows you to write promise-based asynchronous code that reads like synchronous sequential code!

```javascript
async function loadDashboard() {
  console.log("Loading dashboard...");

  // 'await' pauses execution until the promise resolves!
  const user = await fetchUserData(1);
  console.log(`Welcome back, ${user.name}!`);

  return "Dashboard Ready";
}

loadDashboard();
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
function simulateFetch(data, delayMs) {
  return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(data), delayMs));
}

async function loadDashboard() {
  console.log("Step 1: Requesting user...");
  const user = await simulateFetch({ name: "Elena", role: "Admin" }, 50);
  console.log("Step 2: User received:", user.name);
  return `Dashboard for ${user.name} loaded.`;
}

loadDashboard().then(status => console.log("Status:", status));
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 💡 **Notice:** `await` can only be used inside functions marked with the `async` keyword (or top-level in ES modules).

# Error Handling with `try...catch` 🛡️

Handle errors in `async`/`await` using standard `try...catch` blocks:

```javascript
async function executeTask() {
  try {
    const result = await riskyNetworkCall();
    console.log("Result:", result);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Caught network error:", error.message);
  } finally {
    console.log("Finished request lifecycle.");
  }
}
```

