# Parallel Execution: `Promise.all()` 🚀

When you have multiple independent promises, running them sequentially with `await` wastes time. `Promise.all()` runs them concurrently in parallel!

```javascript
const fetchUsers = Promise.resolve(["Alice", "Bob"]);
const fetchProducts = Promise.resolve(["Laptop", "Mouse"]);

// Runs concurrently!
const [users, products] = await Promise.all([fetchUsers, fetchProducts]);

console.log("Users:", users);
console.log("Products:", products);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const p1 = Promise.resolve("Users Data");
const p2 = Promise.resolve("Analytics Data");
const p3 = Promise.resolve("Notifications Data");

Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]).then(([users, analytics, notifs]) => {
  console.log("All parallel promises resolved:");
  console.log("-", users);
  console.log("-", analytics);
  console.log("-", notifs);
});
FULL_CODE_END -->

> ⚠️ **Fail-Fast Behavior:** If even **one** promise in `Promise.all()` rejects, the entire `Promise.all()` rejects immediately!

# Resilient Parallelism: `Promise.allSettled()` 🛡️

Unlike `Promise.all()`, `Promise.allSettled()` **never rejects**! It waits for all promises to finish and returns an array showing the outcome of each:

```javascript
const p1 = Promise.resolve("Success 1");
const p2 = Promise.reject(new Error("Failed 2"));

const results = await Promise.allSettled([p1, p2]);
console.log(results);
// [
//   { status: 'fulfilled', value: 'Success 1' },
//   { status: 'rejected', reason: Error: Failed 2 }
// ]
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const p1 = Promise.resolve("Success 1");
const p2 = Promise.reject(new Error("Failed 2"));

Promise.allSettled([p1, p2]).then(results => {
  results.forEach((res, i) => {
    if (res.status === 'fulfilled') {
      console.log(`Promise ${i+1}: Fulfilled with value "${res.value}"`);
    } else {
      console.log(`Promise ${i+1}: Rejected with reason "${res.reason.message}"`);
    }
